18417	18764	18305	18306	his father	466	" That 's all very good on your part , father , but we must argue the point a little . If you are as great a philosopher as Abraham , I am not quite so dutiful a son as Isaac , whose blind obedience , in my opinion , is very contrary to your rights of man : but the fact , in few words , is simply this . In promulgating your philosophy , in the short space of two days , I have been robbed of the fish I caught , and my rod and line -- I have been soused into a fish - pond -- I have been frightened out of my wits by a bull - dog -- been nearly killed by a bull -- been stung to death by bees , and twice tumbled into a well . Now , if all that happens in two days , what must I expect to suffer in a whole year ? It appears to be very unwise to attempt making further converts , for people on shore seem determined not to listen to reason or argument . But it has occurred to me , that although the whole earth has been so nefariously divided among the few , that the waters at least are the property of all . No man claims his share of the sea -- every one may there plough as he pleases , without being taken up for a trespasser . Even war makes no difference ; every one may go on as he pleases , and if they meet , it is nothing but a neutral ground on which the parties contend . It is , then , only upon the ocean that I am likely to find that equality and rights of man , which we are so anxious to establish on shore ; and therefore I have resolved not to go to school again , which I detest , but to go to sea , and propagate our opinions as much as I can . "
